  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-5+5\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg\u2061(z)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/assets/f466a110eda3b07b50d988de6e46a541ad85d7ff64f43a6f056ea216752fa2d9.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg\u2061(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan\u2061\\alpha  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{5} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\u2061(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg\u2061(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$",
